{"hash":"e94b8e8b858592aa","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":112.874,"said":"The Shiva Purana tells how the mountain Vindhya shaped a linga out of earth and worshipped it here. That humble form of worship, the parthiv linga, a linga of clay, has never stopped on these banks, and one woman gave it new life. Ahilyabai Holkar ruled Malwa in the eighteenth century from Maheshwar, a little way down this same river. Her devotion to Shiva was such that her orders went out not over her own name but as ‘Shri Shankar Aadesh’, the command of Lord Shankar. In Maheshwar she began a daily worship of clay lingas, and at Mamleshwar, across the river from this temple, Brahmins paid by the Holkar state performed lingarchan every day. Each priest had a wooden board with about thirteen hundred tiny hollows. Into each he pressed a little linga of sacred clay, no bigger than a betel nut, and worshipped them all to the chanting of the Rudra. Then the lingas were given back to the Narmada. It is said that grains were placed inside them, so that even the creatures of the deep river would be fed. Temple memory tells of one day when the queen worshipped thirteen thousand such lingas. The custom lives on. A trust in her name still has parthiv lingas made and worshipped at Mamleshwar, and here at Omkareshwar a devotee can offer a Partheshwar puja of 1,325 clay lingas on a single board. In this age, the priests say, a linga of earth pleases Shiva most of all. Clay to clay, river to river: nothing is kept, everything is offered.\n\nDid you know? An old saying in the tradition holds that lingas of gold were worshipped in the Satya Yuga, of mercury in the Treta and of silver in the Dvapara, but in the Kali Yuga a linga of simple clay bears the greatest fruit.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1446},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1462,"end":1677}],"words":[[0.098,0.12,0,3],[0.23,0.349,4,9],[0.591,0.457,10,16],[1.06,0.409,17,22],[1.48,0.264,23,26],[1.757,0.108,27,30],[1.